Changed the fallback branch of the generated `__getattr__` in `src/attr/_make.py:619-627` (for classes without an original `__getattr__`):

```python
try:
    return super().__getattribute__(item)
except AttributeError:
    if not hasattr(super(), '__getattr__'):
        raise
    return super().__getattr__(item)
```

Now an `AttributeError` raised inside a cached property's body (e.g. from a plain `@property`) propagates unchanged instead of being replaced by a synthesized "object has no attribute" message, while a base-class `__getattr__` is still consulted when present. Missing attributes still raise the standard `AttributeError` from `object.__getattribute__`.

